# Colour

Every colour on a page comes from a named token. Components reference
tokens, never raw values, so light and dark themes work without
per-component effort.

## Base palette

basecoat-css ships the structural palette as CSS custom properties,
each with a `-foreground` pair for text rendered on top of it:

| Token | Use |
| ----- | --- |
| `--background` / `--foreground` | Page surface and default text |
| `--card` | Card and panel surfaces |
| `--popover` | Floating surfaces (menus, popovers, toasts) |
| `--primary` | Primary actions and emphasis |
| `--secondary` | Secondary actions |
| `--muted` | De-emphasised surfaces and text |
| `--accent` | Hover/active tinting |
| `--destructive` | Destructive actions |
| `--border` / `--input` / `--ring` | Borders, field borders, focus ring |
| `--sidebar-*` | Sidebar surface, text, accent, border |

Tailwind utilities are generated for each (`bg-primary`,
`text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`, …).

## Sidebar accent

`--sidebar-primary` (with its `-foreground` pair) is the one brand
accent: a blue-violet, `oklch(0.488 0.243 264.376)`, used for the
active sidebar item and the header avatar fallback. basecoat ships
it neutral in light mode; `theme.css` overrides it so both themes
match the React boilerplate. It belongs to the shell — don't reach
for it in page content, where `--primary` and the state tokens
carry emphasis.

## Clinical state tokens

The NHS UI adds five semantic state colours in
`app/assets/tailwind/theme.css`, ported from the React boilerplate.
Values are OKLCH and theme-aware (light in `:root`, dark in `.dark`);
each has a `-foreground` pair and generated utilities (`bg-warning`,
`text-info`, …).

| Token | Meaning | Example use |
| ----- | ------- | ----------- |
| `--success` | Positive outcome, completed state | success toast, positive stat-card badge |
| `--info` | Neutral information | info alert, info badge |
| `--warning` | Needs attention, not yet harmful | warning alert, overdue badge |
| `--critical` | Harmful or blocking; strongest signal | critical alert, error toast |
| `--stale` | Data past its freshness window | stale-data badge |

`--critical` is the clinical-severity counterpart to basecoat's
`--destructive`: use `--destructive` for destructive *actions*
(delete buttons) and `--critical` for critical *states* (alerts,
toasts, badges).

## Rules

- Never hard-code a colour value in a component or page — extend
  `theme.css` if a genuinely new semantic colour is needed (rare).
- Pick tokens by meaning, not appearance. A badge is `--warning`
  because the data needs attention, not because orange looks right.
- Always pair a surface token with its `-foreground` for text on it.
- State colours signal state; they are not decoration. A page should
  read calmly in neutrals with state colour drawing the eye only to
  what needs it.
- Both themes come free if tokens are used; check dark mode anyway
  when composing dense state-colour UIs (the theme toggle is in the
  header).
